rogram it hardly seems fair does it  that you know olive oil exporters or  exporters of whiskey or exporters of  suits or whatever it might be that have  nothing to do with Boeing or Airbus  should be caught in the crossfire here  well yes I can understand why that  people involved might might see it that  way the strange thing about these  tariffs is that they have gone through  the WTO insofar as the WTO green-lighted  the Trump administration to impose them  having made a judgment on the case about  the subsidies to Airbus so I can see how  the people involved might rightly think  they're unrelated to that case but they  have gone through a system external to  the US and a way some of the other  tariffs that been imposed by Washington  haven't  well after that WTO ruling of course  there was a lot of discussion even  coming from the United States that said  look we'd rather find a negotiated  settlement here and that those tack that  those subsidies should be withdrawn from  from Europe when it comes to Airbus but  why didn't a negotiated process succeed  and  have been necessary to wrap this up now  well it's a very long running dispute  and of course it goes both ways so it's  all very well for the u.s. to say that  they'd be happy if the subsidies the  Airbus were wound up but of course the  European Union wants America to provide  less support to Boeing there's no doubt  really that both sides are subsidizing  at these domestic industries and it's  entirely possible that the WTO will rule  that the EU is allowed to impose tariffs  on American goods - the only thing that  might hold that up is that America is  holding up nominations to the WTO's  Appeals Board so because if that's  gumming up the WTO a bit that might get  in the way but we're not for that most  people expect that the WTO would make an  equivalent judgement in the opposite  direction it's the idea that this is a  sort of small dispute that could be  negotiated away easily is wrong it's  very long-running and it has multiple  parts and Henry if that happens if the  WTO and it did rule in favor of as you  said it ruled in favor of the year of  the US tariffs if the WTO said okay now  Europe can impose their own we're into a  tit-for-tat trade war aren't we and what  are the the consequences of that more  broadly  well we yes we would be of course the  purpose of the WTO procedures is to try  and constrain tit-for-tat trade wars to  try and make sure that all tariff  responses are proportionate the  complaints that are brought I think that  bigger concern here is that we're  operating at a time now where the Trump  administration is perfectly willing to  circumvent the WTO so although in this  case it has been approved there's still  this outstanding matter of whether  America might impose tariffs on European  cars for instance in the name of  national security that's still basically  an option available to the Trump  administration and the president has on  multiple occasions attacked European  trade policy he's annoyed with the  European Central Bank for its loose  monetary policy which he sees as keeping  the euro too weak and so on so I think  the question is whether this spills  beyond the matter of aircraft making and  into a brawl  trade dispute that's not constrained  within the WTO procedures and all of  this of course at a time of slowing  growth in the ongoing trade war with  China as well Henry thank you so much  for giving us your time thank you thank  you
 

